<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salzwedel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salzwedel is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Jeetze river. A fortified settlement on a salt route by the early 12th century, it belonged to the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and prospered from trade. Its preserved medieval old town is known for brick Gothic churches and for Baumkuchen, the layered 'tree cake'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salzwedel.jpg" alt="Salzwedel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Björn Gäde at German Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salzwedel_Fachwerk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
